WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.131 --> 00:00:02.040
- Never thought about a career.

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.160
Never written a note of music for money.

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:05.360
We're experimenting.

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.902
(drums beating rhythmically)

00:00:09.902 --> 00:00:11.991
Mighty big, sounds awesome.

00:00:11.991 --> 00:00:15.161
Black Hawk Down I remember
being up for 72 hours.

00:00:15.161 --> 00:00:17.767
Nobody asked me to be up for 72 hours.

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:19.874
Nobody was throwing anything, you know?

00:00:19.874 --> 00:00:21.563
It was fine.

00:00:21.563 --> 00:00:25.512
Thank you to the people of
Ghent for not killing me.

00:00:25.512 --> 00:00:27.660
There's something very human
about it because it can

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.827
only make a sound with air
and it needs to breathe.

00:00:32.912 --> 00:00:35.525
- He's a German composer
and record producer.

00:00:35.525 --> 00:00:38.079
He's the head of the film
music division at Dreamworks.

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:41.712
He's composed the music for
over 150 films, including

00:00:41.712 --> 00:00:44.663
Lion King, Gladiator, and
Pirates of the Caribbean.

00:00:44.663 --> 00:00:46.265
He's Hans Zimmer, and here's my take

00:00:46.265 --> 00:00:48.123
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:48.123 --> 00:00:50.120
Rule number eight is my personal favorite.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:51.462
Make sure to stick around all the way to

00:00:51.462 --> 00:00:53.403
the end for some special bonus clips.

00:00:53.403 --> 00:00:55.369
Also, as Hans is talking,
if he says something

00:00:55.369 --> 00:00:56.939
that really, really resonates with you,

00:00:56.939 --> 00:00:58.789
please leave it in the comments below.

00:00:58.789 --> 00:00:59.890
Put quotes around it so other

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:01.584
people can be inspired as well.

00:01:01.584 --> 00:01:02.642
Enjoy.

00:01:02.642 --> 00:01:05.337
(whooshing tone)

00:01:05.337 --> 00:01:08.254
(determined music)

00:01:15.337 --> 00:01:19.137
(suspenseful music)

00:01:19.137 --> 00:01:23.304
- Guy from Germany who stubbornly
just wanted to play music.

00:01:25.327 --> 00:01:27.994
- Hans Zimmer is a crazy genius.

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:30.349
- He's just one of the nicest people

00:01:30.349 --> 00:01:33.239
I've ever met and a great film composer.

00:01:33.239 --> 00:01:34.806
- Never thought about a career.

00:01:34.806 --> 00:01:38.003
Never written a note of music for money.

00:01:38.003 --> 00:01:39.315
- I don't think a few words to

00:01:39.315 --> 00:01:41.355
define Hans the way I know him.

00:01:41.355 --> 00:01:42.803
Hans is a genius.

00:01:42.803 --> 00:01:46.106
- Somebody who's concerned
with making sure that

00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:50.155
we will have orchestras
forever and try to support my

00:01:50.155 --> 00:01:54.322
musician friends and try
to discover new people and

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:56.812
never growing up.

00:01:56.812 --> 00:01:59.222
- A lot of the producers
go, "Oh my God, no, Hans,

00:01:59.222 --> 00:02:00.636
can you not write the thing that you did

00:02:00.636 --> 00:02:02.626
so that you get an
Oscar," and this and that.

00:02:02.626 --> 00:02:05.035
And he said, "No, I'm going
to do it all differently."

00:02:05.035 --> 00:02:06.349
And everybody's scared.

00:02:06.349 --> 00:02:08.914
And in the end it's a hit.

00:02:08.914 --> 00:02:11.479
And in the end, everybody
copies Hans' music.

00:02:11.479 --> 00:02:14.729
- He's this guy that I met 37 years ago

00:02:17.773 --> 00:02:21.226
that's written some unbelievable music

00:02:21.226 --> 00:02:23.643
and had an incredible career.

00:02:25.153 --> 00:02:26.678
- I play music.

00:02:26.678 --> 00:02:28.077
That's what I do.

00:02:28.077 --> 00:02:30.990
After finishing my
adventures on Dark Knight,

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:32.757
I decided we need to go into

00:02:32.757 --> 00:02:34.625
a completely different direction.

00:02:34.625 --> 00:02:38.375
(energetic orchestral music)

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:43.236
While I think of the notes for the tune,

00:02:43.236 --> 00:02:46.641
I try to think of what's the (mumbles)

00:02:46.641 --> 00:02:51.256
pour this tune into, and
that's what we're doing.

00:02:51.256 --> 00:02:55.005
Basses, can you make your
accents a bit more fierce?

00:02:55.005 --> 00:02:56.145
We're experimenting.

00:02:56.145 --> 00:02:59.895
(drums beating rhythmically)

00:03:00.854 --> 00:03:02.022
Mighty big.

00:03:02.022 --> 00:03:03.770
Sounds awesome.

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:04.788
That's what you're supposed to do.

00:03:04.788 --> 00:03:07.129
You're not supposed to stand still.

00:03:07.129 --> 00:03:10.406
You don't really want to be observed

00:03:10.406 --> 00:03:12.787
at your greatest failures.

00:03:12.787 --> 00:03:15.186
You have to invite failure if
you want to go and experiment

00:03:15.186 --> 00:03:17.474
and if you want to try
to do something original.

00:03:17.474 --> 00:03:18.742
We have to have a certain amount of

00:03:18.742 --> 00:03:20.974
privacy to have the courage to fail.

00:03:20.974 --> 00:03:24.693
Black Hawk Down or any of
those films we worked on,

00:03:24.693 --> 00:03:26.443
nobody asked us to...

00:03:27.426 --> 00:03:30.526
Black Hawk Down, I remember
being up for 72 hours.

00:03:30.526 --> 00:03:33.293
Nobody asked me to be up for 72 hours.

00:03:33.293 --> 00:03:36.509
I was trying to do
something extraordinary.

00:03:36.509 --> 00:03:38.994
Trying to tell a story
in an extraordinary way.

00:03:38.994 --> 00:03:39.827
Trying to

00:03:44.556 --> 00:03:46.221
be a band of brothers.

00:03:46.221 --> 00:03:49.889
Not let anybody else down
that was on your team.

00:03:49.889 --> 00:03:51.699
- [Interviewer] Be superhuman.

00:03:51.699 --> 00:03:52.763
- It's not superhuman.

00:03:52.763 --> 00:03:54.214
It's human.

00:03:54.214 --> 00:03:56.957
The unfortunate thing is
that you have to put the word

00:03:56.957 --> 00:04:01.027
super in front of it
when it's the ambition.

00:04:01.027 --> 00:04:03.944
It's what we should all strive for.

00:04:05.061 --> 00:04:07.691
That commitment to the
gesture, to the love,

00:04:07.691 --> 00:04:11.858
to how we approach the day,
how we approach each other,

00:04:13.137 --> 00:04:16.155
how we approach humanity, how we approach

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:20.155
art and beauty and
ultimately, in Martin's case,

00:04:21.953 --> 00:04:24.692
how we approach our instrument.

00:04:24.692 --> 00:04:26.859
There needs to be respect.

00:04:27.756 --> 00:04:30.624
At the end of the day,
I don't think the issue

00:04:30.624 --> 00:04:34.395
really is about the recording medium,

00:04:34.395 --> 00:04:38.376
but it is about the
players and the whole team.

00:04:38.376 --> 00:04:42.543
I think you're not going to
get quite the same results

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:49.145
by having your mate in the
flat next door go and bang

00:04:49.145 --> 00:04:52.365
on a biscuit tin, even though
it might be interesting,

00:04:52.365 --> 00:04:56.703
than if you get the best
players in the world

00:04:56.703 --> 00:04:59.286
to go and know where to hit it.

00:05:00.991 --> 00:05:01.824
You know what I mean?

00:05:01.824 --> 00:05:05.241
A drum is this interesting archaic thing,

00:05:07.114 --> 00:05:11.281
but there are sweet spots
when you hit it just right.

00:05:12.675 --> 00:05:15.035
These guys have been doing it forever.

00:05:15.035 --> 00:05:20.002
So it's really about the human
beings that go and create

00:05:20.002 --> 00:05:23.078
the sound that ultimately will
tell you if it's any good.

00:05:23.078 --> 00:05:26.273
The Lion King was, of course, it was huge,

00:05:26.273 --> 00:05:28.597
especially because I did it
for all the wrong reasons.

00:05:28.597 --> 00:05:31.160
I did it for my daughter because

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:33.581
I wanted to show off as dad.

00:05:33.581 --> 00:05:35.851
I was doing all these grown
up movies where people were

00:05:35.851 --> 00:05:38.688
getting killed and I couldn't
take her to a premiere.

00:05:38.688 --> 00:05:42.855
I wanted to do something to
take my kid to a premiere.

00:05:45.516 --> 00:05:48.854
It was surprisingly
emotional doing that movie.

00:05:48.854 --> 00:05:51.687
It deals with a father's death, so

00:05:54.614 --> 00:05:58.781
it's got a lot more depth than
the surface let me believe.

00:05:59.752 --> 00:06:03.568
And it shocked me, my own
response once I started writing

00:06:03.568 --> 00:06:07.735
on it because I was thinking
this is going to be easy.

00:06:10.143 --> 00:06:12.497
And it wasn't like it was hard,

00:06:12.497 --> 00:06:14.759
but the emotional involvement suddenly

00:06:14.759 --> 00:06:17.676
became profound as opposed to hard.

00:06:22.467 --> 00:06:25.139
And really it was Lion
King that got Terry Malick

00:06:25.139 --> 00:06:27.839
interested in me, for instance.

00:06:27.839 --> 00:06:32.006
Terry loved Lion King and
wanted me to do Thin Red Line.

00:06:33.293 --> 00:06:38.192
Working with Ridley Scott,
we've done some really good

00:06:38.192 --> 00:06:40.906
movies together and they're
stylistically so different.

00:06:40.906 --> 00:06:43.497
I mean, Black Rain is so
different from Thelma and Louise.

00:06:43.497 --> 00:06:45.497
It's so different from Gladiator.

00:06:45.497 --> 00:06:46.539
So different from Hannibal.

00:06:46.539 --> 00:06:48.787
It's so different from Black Hawk Down.

00:06:48.787 --> 00:06:50.693
It's so different from Matchstick Men.

00:06:50.693 --> 00:06:53.193
I'm sure I'm leaving some out.

00:06:54.623 --> 00:06:58.206
So working with people
who are like me that

00:06:59.589 --> 00:07:01.747
get a little bored doing the same thing

00:07:01.747 --> 00:07:05.914
and off they go to try to do
something completely different,

00:07:10.744 --> 00:07:12.554
that's a big part of it.

00:07:12.554 --> 00:07:14.989
When I started, people
were cutting on Steenbecks

00:07:14.989 --> 00:07:17.989
and KEMs and then they got the Avid.

00:07:19.101 --> 00:07:22.076
The interesting thing
about a sound (mumbles) is

00:07:22.076 --> 00:07:24.529
because sound is slightly
more easy to handle

00:07:24.529 --> 00:07:28.571
in large quantities of data than images.

00:07:28.571 --> 00:07:31.651
We've always been ahead of the image guys.

00:07:31.651 --> 00:07:33.853
I mean of the film guys.

00:07:33.853 --> 00:07:37.686
So we've made all those
mistakes with digital.

00:07:39.033 --> 00:07:42.033
The old digital recordings sound bad

00:07:43.289 --> 00:07:44.868
and it wasn't just the technology.

00:07:44.868 --> 00:07:47.285
We didn't know how to use it.

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:51.897
Part of that is just you
have to put your hours in.

00:07:51.897 --> 00:07:54.578
I'm a real believer in that thing,

00:07:54.578 --> 00:07:57.450
it's 10,000 hours to
get good at something.

00:07:57.450 --> 00:07:58.473
- [Interviewer] Is that the big difference

00:07:58.473 --> 00:08:01.254
because I see you in your basement like

00:08:01.254 --> 00:08:03.745
a Batman with all machines and stuff.

00:08:03.745 --> 00:08:06.162
It's really precise, I guess.

00:08:07.389 --> 00:08:10.043
You know what you put
and then you go live.

00:08:10.043 --> 00:08:11.254
What's the difference?

00:08:11.254 --> 00:08:12.306
The risk?
- Energy.

00:08:12.306 --> 00:08:13.139
The risk.

00:08:16.226 --> 00:08:20.393
I have stage fright and I
never confronted the risk.

00:08:21.761 --> 00:08:23.608
I mean, Belgium.

00:08:23.608 --> 00:08:24.441
Year 2000.

00:08:25.492 --> 00:08:30.455
You have no idea what it
took then to get me to do it.

00:08:30.455 --> 00:08:34.609
No, no, no, no, I had 1,000
excuses why not to do it,

00:08:34.609 --> 00:08:37.609
and then finally I did it and it was

00:08:42.202 --> 00:08:44.227
exquisitely horrifying.

00:08:44.227 --> 00:08:45.693
That's the only way I can put it.

00:08:45.693 --> 00:08:46.526
- [Interviewer] Complex.

00:08:46.526 --> 00:08:50.276
- Yes, it was very,
very, very, very complex.

00:08:51.515 --> 00:08:54.809
What was really good was
right at the beginning

00:08:54.809 --> 00:08:58.976
within two seconds, I played
just the worst wrong note.

00:09:00.656 --> 00:09:05.494
I could either stop and cry
or I could laugh, right?

00:09:05.494 --> 00:09:07.933
And it didn't kill me,
and nobody else out there.

00:09:07.933 --> 00:09:09.897
Nobody was throwing anything.

00:09:09.897 --> 00:09:10.897
It was fine.

00:09:11.761 --> 00:09:15.743
Thank you to the people of
Ghent for not killing me

00:09:15.743 --> 00:09:19.493
and giving me the courage,
and the absurdity.

00:09:20.604 --> 00:09:23.290
You have to be able to laugh at yourself

00:09:23.290 --> 00:09:26.610
and you have to learn
how to laugh at yourself.

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:29.698
The greatest compliment
I think I've ever gotten,

00:09:29.698 --> 00:09:31.955
better than an Oscar, better
than a Grammy, et cetera,

00:09:31.955 --> 00:09:36.122
was towards the end of the
last Dark Knight Rises.

00:09:38.082 --> 00:09:41.372
Working with those percussionists
that we've just sampled,

00:09:41.372 --> 00:09:43.656
these guys have played on everything.

00:09:43.656 --> 00:09:45.156
They are the guys.

00:09:46.212 --> 00:09:48.339
There's nothing they haven't played.

00:09:48.339 --> 00:09:52.950
So my great ambition has
always been to go and do just

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:57.472
one thing where they go,
wow, never done that before.

00:09:57.472 --> 00:09:58.497
I actually figured something.

00:09:58.497 --> 00:10:00.333
I'm not going to go into the details,

00:10:00.333 --> 00:10:03.467
but number one, I got
the compliment of going,

00:10:03.467 --> 00:10:05.018
"Wow, we've never done this before

00:10:05.018 --> 00:10:06.862
"and it sounds really good."

00:10:06.862 --> 00:10:08.210
I was terrible at school.

00:10:08.210 --> 00:10:10.252
I never managed to go to university.

00:10:10.252 --> 00:10:14.243
But I started working for
another film composer.

00:10:14.243 --> 00:10:18.542
What happened literally on
day one, I am in the room.

00:10:18.542 --> 00:10:20.441
My job is to make coffee.

00:10:20.441 --> 00:10:24.956
But I'm in a room with
Nick Roeg, who I adore

00:10:24.956 --> 00:10:28.176
as a director, and Stanley
Myers, who I adored

00:10:28.176 --> 00:10:32.343
as a composer, and they're
talking about the scene.

00:10:33.218 --> 00:10:35.115
There's a real live problem.

00:10:35.115 --> 00:10:39.060
How are we going to score
the scene as opposed to

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:41.976
what I experienced in
school, which is somebody

00:10:41.976 --> 00:10:46.108
tells you some historical
incident that then got solved.

00:10:46.108 --> 00:10:50.275
So the most important part of
the mentorship was not only

00:10:51.658 --> 00:10:54.243
was I in the room with a
problem that had not been solved

00:10:54.243 --> 00:10:57.364
and I was witnessing how this
problem in front of my eyes

00:10:57.364 --> 00:10:59.915
got solved by Stanley going,
"Oh, what about those notes,"

00:10:59.915 --> 00:11:01.963
and Nick going, "That's exactly it,"

00:11:01.963 --> 00:11:04.963
but just being in a room with people

00:11:06.835 --> 00:11:09.668
who were the best at what the did.

00:11:11.727 --> 00:11:14.498
In a funny way I thought everybody

00:11:14.498 --> 00:11:16.998
should have the right to this.

00:11:18.499 --> 00:11:20.999
So I try to make this place...

00:11:22.789 --> 00:11:24.292
This is a big room.

00:11:24.292 --> 00:11:27.309
When it's not full of Dark Knight synth,

00:11:27.309 --> 00:11:30.086
there's actually a lot of room in here.

00:11:30.086 --> 00:11:34.919
I think something really
good happens if you can be.

00:11:34.919 --> 00:11:36.206
You know, it's your first day.

00:11:36.206 --> 00:11:37.731
You're here to make coffee, but you're in

00:11:37.731 --> 00:11:39.827
the room with Chris Nolan and Hans Zimmer

00:11:39.827 --> 00:11:43.538
and you're seeing them
scratching their head.

00:11:43.538 --> 00:11:46.362
Somehow, even if you
don't open your mouth,

00:11:46.362 --> 00:11:49.147
just by your presence,
you're part of the solution,

00:11:49.147 --> 00:11:50.429
or at least you get to see it.

00:11:50.429 --> 00:11:52.752
This is, of course,
where Chris gets tricky.

00:11:52.752 --> 00:11:56.045
Really about a father and
his relationship to his son.

00:11:56.045 --> 00:11:57.566
It's only later on that I found out

00:11:57.566 --> 00:11:59.774
that the son wasn't the son at all.

00:11:59.774 --> 00:12:01.249
It was a daughter.

00:12:01.249 --> 00:12:04.280
(solemn music)

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:07.622
I sat down and I wrote this
piece really about what it feels

00:12:07.622 --> 00:12:10.509
like to be a father and what
it feels like to have a son.

00:12:10.509 --> 00:12:12.759
I was writing about my son.

00:12:13.802 --> 00:12:15.487
- When he played me the piece of music,

00:12:15.487 --> 00:12:17.636
which became the basis
for the entire score,

00:12:17.636 --> 00:12:19.554
I thought it was absolutely
perfect and captured

00:12:19.554 --> 00:12:23.008
the emotional qualities
of the film that I wanted.

00:12:23.008 --> 00:12:24.370
It was at that point that
I told him that it was

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:26.751
actually a large scale
science fiction film.

00:12:26.751 --> 00:12:28.748
I hadn't given him any clues about that.

00:12:28.748 --> 00:12:30.189
You know, it's not an action film.

00:12:30.189 --> 00:12:34.493
It's not about (background
music drowns out speaker).

00:12:34.493 --> 00:12:37.276
I think what that did for
him is it set him very firmly

00:12:37.276 --> 00:12:40.624
in a direction related
to the heart of the film.

00:12:40.624 --> 00:12:43.616
I listened to it extensively
while I was finishing writing

00:12:43.616 --> 00:12:47.290
the script and then when we
were in production as well.

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:50.686
- The organ really is a huge,
complicated synthesizer.

00:12:50.686 --> 00:12:53.720
If you think about it, you
have a pipe and air blows

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:55.543
through it and that makes a sound,

00:12:55.543 --> 00:12:58.711
and it makes the sound of one pitch.

00:12:58.711 --> 00:13:00.336
And then if you want to shift color,

00:13:00.336 --> 00:13:03.092
you add another pipe to
it and you add more pipes.

00:13:03.092 --> 00:13:05.254
And so it becomes these really very,

00:13:05.254 --> 00:13:08.004
very complex harmonic structures.

00:13:09.005 --> 00:13:11.402
There was this endless discovery

00:13:11.402 --> 00:13:13.758
going on for both Chris and me where

00:13:13.758 --> 00:13:16.357
Roger would take us through
all these different voicings

00:13:16.357 --> 00:13:17.819
and all these different colors.

00:13:17.819 --> 00:13:20.998
You can even go a little darker.

00:13:20.998 --> 00:13:24.581
Actually, then we should
go for this sound.

00:13:25.452 --> 00:13:27.643
There's something very human
about it because it can

00:13:27.643 --> 00:13:31.810
only make a sound with air
and it needs to breathe.

00:13:33.556 --> 00:13:36.389
On each note, you hear the breath.

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:39.487
You hear the exhale.

00:13:40.706 --> 00:13:45.216
- You feel a human presence in
every sound and I think that

00:13:45.216 --> 00:13:48.680
was very important to keeping
the film about not just the

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:53.387
space that we're looking at,
but the people in that space.

00:13:53.387 --> 00:13:56.289
There's an intimacy as
well as massive scale.

00:13:56.289 --> 00:13:59.593
Sometimes within a few
bars, there's that shift.

00:13:59.593 --> 00:14:03.260
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:14:12.826 --> 00:14:14.056
Pulling out all the stops.

00:14:14.056 --> 00:14:17.973
I now know what that
expression means for real.

00:14:19.714 --> 00:14:20.942
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:14:20.942 --> 00:14:24.394
I made this video because
David Dumais asked me to.

00:14:24.394 --> 00:14:25.957
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you

00:14:25.957 --> 00:14:28.098
want me to profile next,
leave it in the comments

00:14:28.098 --> 00:14:29.525
below and I'll see what I can do.

00:14:29.525 --> 00:14:31.021
I'd also love to know which rule

00:14:31.021 --> 00:14:32.514
had the biggest impact on you.

00:14:32.514 --> 00:14:35.106
What did Hans say that is
going to have an impact

00:14:35.106 --> 00:14:36.867
on your life or business somehow?

00:14:36.867 --> 00:14:38.390
Leave it in the comments below.

00:14:38.390 --> 00:14:40.185
I'm going to join into the discussion.

00:14:40.185 --> 00:14:42.492
Finally, I want to give a quick
shout out to Fred McCormack.

00:14:42.492 --> 00:14:43.885
Fred, thank you so much for picking up

00:14:43.885 --> 00:14:45.909
a copy of my book, Your One Word.

00:14:45.909 --> 00:14:48.220
It really, really, really,
really means a lot to me.

00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:49.546
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:14:49.546 --> 00:14:50.802
I believe in you.

00:14:50.802 --> 00:14:53.146
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:14:53.146 --> 00:14:55.585
and whatever your one word is.

00:14:55.585 --> 00:14:56.656
Much love.

00:14:56.656 --> 00:14:58.336
I'll see you soon.

00:14:58.336 --> 00:15:01.086
(whooshing tone)

00:15:04.123 --> 00:15:06.565
- I think the only thing
that can save us at the end

00:15:06.565 --> 00:15:09.256
of the day as a human race is science.

00:15:09.256 --> 00:15:11.132
The only thing that inspires science

00:15:11.132 --> 00:15:13.215
all the time is dreamers.

00:15:15.397 --> 00:15:16.859
Would we have gotten to the moon without

00:15:16.859 --> 00:15:20.442
(mumbles) or Isaac Asimov.

00:15:21.383 --> 00:15:24.376
I don't know good design that's...

00:15:24.376 --> 00:15:27.155
- [Interviewer] So you're hopeful
because we still have art?

00:15:27.155 --> 00:15:28.110
Heart and art?

00:15:28.110 --> 00:15:29.574
- I think I'm hopeful.

00:15:29.574 --> 00:15:32.846
I'm hopeful because I'm a musician.

00:15:32.846 --> 00:15:37.236
I'm realizing this more
and more what happens is

00:15:37.236 --> 00:15:39.691
it's not that we can play really well.

00:15:39.691 --> 00:15:41.011
We learn how to listen.

00:15:41.011 --> 00:15:42.468
We learn how to listen to each other.

00:15:42.468 --> 00:15:45.299
So we can come from
totally different cultures.

00:15:45.299 --> 00:15:49.903
We might not be able to speak
the same words as language.

00:15:49.903 --> 00:15:51.847
But when we all get
together, if I got a bunch

00:15:51.847 --> 00:15:56.090
of musicians into this room
right now, we'd start playing.

00:15:56.090 --> 00:15:57.063
The first thing we would do is

00:15:57.063 --> 00:15:58.951
we would listen to each other.

00:15:58.951 --> 00:16:01.618
There comes respect and empathy.

00:16:02.912 --> 00:16:04.620
The only way that music can get beautiful

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:06.689
is if we listen to each other.

00:16:06.689 --> 00:16:09.939
I think it's something some politicians

00:16:10.946 --> 00:16:13.484
can learn a little something from.

00:16:13.484 --> 00:16:16.604
All politicians should be
forced to learn an instrument

00:16:16.604 --> 00:16:19.571
and have to jam together every weekend.

00:16:19.571 --> 00:16:20.775
- [Interviewer] And listen to each other.

00:16:20.775 --> 00:16:23.918
- And listen to each other
because you got to shut up.

00:16:23.918 --> 00:16:25.501
You can't be right.

00:16:26.911 --> 00:16:28.740
It's indefensible music.

00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:30.244
It's not about being right or wrong.

00:16:30.244 --> 00:16:34.239
There are no right notes,
they're no wrong notes.

00:16:34.239 --> 00:16:36.457
But it's like, I throw you a note,

00:16:36.457 --> 00:16:38.957
what are you going to do with it?

00:16:44.626 --> 00:16:46.440
I'm not trying to be pretentious about it,

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:49.523
but the listening part is quite good.

00:16:51.481 --> 00:16:53.084
It's not unimportant.

00:16:53.084 --> 00:16:57.612
Sampling isn't the reason
that concert halls are empty.

00:16:57.612 --> 00:17:00.258
What our job is is to drive music forward,

00:17:00.258 --> 00:17:04.212
to make it interesting, to
make it commercial in the best

00:17:04.212 --> 00:17:08.524
possible sense, to say an
orchestra can rock out.

00:17:08.524 --> 00:17:10.385
You can go and see an
orchestral performance

00:17:10.385 --> 00:17:12.361
and it'll knock your socks off.

00:17:12.361 --> 00:17:17.062
I'm just interested in seeing
how far we can push that.

00:17:17.062 --> 00:17:21.433
Movies is one way,
sampling is another way.

00:17:21.433 --> 00:17:24.100
Life performance is another way.

00:17:25.873 --> 00:17:28.434
It's one of the reasons
I love working with

00:17:28.434 --> 00:17:32.466
(mumbles) because he does
these shows in front of

00:17:32.466 --> 00:17:35.133
250,000 people and they go nuts.

00:17:37.186 --> 00:17:39.757
It's just one man and his synthesizers.

00:17:39.757 --> 00:17:41.338
He's just written his first symphony

00:17:41.338 --> 00:17:44.380
which was performed in Amsterdam.

00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:46.401
Yes, with a full orchestra.

00:17:46.401 --> 00:17:49.901
So these ideas are not mutually exclusive.

00:17:50.999 --> 00:17:53.107
Tools are tools.

00:17:53.107 --> 00:17:55.515
All music relies on technology.

00:17:55.515 --> 00:17:59.682
A violin is just a bit of good
carpentry and some dead cats.

00:18:00.882 --> 00:18:02.798
It does involve a certain amount

00:18:02.798 --> 00:18:05.081
of technology to get you there.

00:18:05.081 --> 00:18:07.801
At the end of the day, it's
the ingenuity of the player

00:18:07.801 --> 00:18:10.211
and it's the ingenuity of
the composer that are going

00:18:10.211 --> 00:18:13.297
to go and really make
something out of this thing.

00:18:13.297 --> 00:18:15.883
Somebody said to me
recently, "When you don't

00:18:15.883 --> 00:18:18.753
"feel the nerves, you have
to go and do something else."

00:18:18.753 --> 00:18:20.679
I feel the nerves, trust me.

00:18:20.679 --> 00:18:24.429
(uplifting orchestral music)

